Revenue Monitoring Report 2025-26 Cabinet","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s41557/q2-revenue-monitoring-report-2025-26-cabinet.pdf","type":"monitoring","type_label":"Monitoring","weight":5,"committee":"Cabinet","meeting_id":2775,"meeting_date":"2025-12-09","published":"2025-12-01","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2025-12-09-cabinet-41557-q2-revenue-monitoring-report-2025-26-cab.pdf"},{"id":500003716,"title":"Independent- Revenue Budget Amendment - SIDs","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500003716/independent-revenue-budget-amendment-sids.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2728,"meeting_date":"2026-02-26","published":"2026-02-18","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-02-26-council-500003716-independent-revenue-budget-amendment-sid.pdf"},{"id":500003717,"title":"Conservative Group - Revenue Budget Amendment - Business rate hardship fund","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500003717/conservative-group-revenue-budget-amendment-business-rate-ha.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2728,"meeting_date":"2026-02-26","published":"2026-02-18","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-02-26-council-500003717-conservative-group-revenue-budget-amendm.pdf"},{"id":500003718,"title":"Conservative Group - Revenue Budget Amendment - free short stay car parking","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500003718/conservative-group-revenue-budget-amendment-free-short-stay.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2728,"meeting_date":"2026-02-26","published":"2026-02-18","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-02-26-council-500003718-conservative-group-revenue-budget-amendm.pdf"},{"id":500003663,"title":"Appendix 1 Property Investment Strategy","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500003663/appendix-1-property-investment-strategy.pdf","type":"other","type_label":"Paper","weight":1,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2728,"meeting_date":"2026-02-26","published":"2026-02-18","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-02-26-council-500003663-appendix-1-property-investment-strategy.pdf"},{"id":500003670,"title":"Appendix 2 - Investment Strategy (Non-Treasury) 2026-27","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500003670/appendix-2-investment-strategy-non-treasury-2026-27.pdf","type":"other","type_label":"Paper","weight":1,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2728,"meeting_date":"2026-02-26","published":"2026-02-18","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-02-26-council-500003670-appendix-2-investment-strategy-non-treas.pdf"},{"id":500004338,"title":"Appointment of Interim Section 151 Officer","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004338/appointment-of-interim-section-151-officer.pdf","type":"other","type_label":"Paper","weight":1,"committee":"Council","meeting_id":2894,"meeting_date":"2026-07-16","published":"2026-07-08","filename":"E07000084-basingstoke-and-deane-2026-07-16-council-500004338-appointment-of-interim-section-151-offic.pdf"}],"notes":{"gss":"E07000084","schema":2,"council":"Basingstoke and Deane","facts":[{"key":"no_external_debt","value":"no treasury management borrowing undertaken in 2025/26, and no interest costs related to treasury management borrowing","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Paragraph 3.4 (p.4) verbatim: 'No treasury management borrowing was undertaken during 2025/26.' Paragraph 12.3 (p.10) verbatim: 'The council did not have any interest costs related to treasury management borrowing.' Our price book returns no borrower on Basingstoke, Basingstoke and Deane, or Deane. Ties."},{"key":"investment_book","value":"\u00a3114.56m at cost on 31 March 2026; average balance \u00a3130.79m for the year","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Chart 1 (p.3) and paragraph 3.3 (p.4). Split 61% short-term/liquidity, 39% long-term. Chart 1 sums exactly to 114.56 and to the Appendix 1 grand total of \u00a3114,559,164."},{"key":"investment_return","value":"4.34% overall vs 4.18% budget and 4.04% average Bank Rate; internal book 4.23% vs 4.00% MUFG benchmark","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Table 2 (p.5) and paragraph 5.9 (p.6). MUFG benchmark sample is 228 councils, Table 4 (p.7). Table 5 (p.10): net treasury income \u00a35.62m against \u00a34.90m budget, \u00a30.72m above."},{"key":"internal_book_shape","value":"\u00a389.56m internally managed at a 263-day weighted average maturity and a 1.00 credit-risk score, against a 228-council benchmark of \u00a367.83m at 52 days and 1.95","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Table 4 (p.7) and paragraph 7.2 (p.8). \u00a389.56m = Gilts 18.90 + Supras 10.06 + LT Loans 10.00 + ST Loans 30.00 + Liquidity Cash 20.60. A book taken deliberately long into a falling-rate environment."},{"key":"la_lending","value":"seven loans out to other councils totalling \u00a340.0m at 4.30%-5.50%, weighted average 4.525%","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Appendix 1 (p.14). Basildon 5m/5m at 4.35/4.55; Central Bedfordshire 5m at 5.50% for 3 months (17 Mar \u2192 12 Jun 2026); Dorset 5m at 4.35; Gravesham 5m at 4.30; Northumberland 10m at 4.30 to 22 Oct 2027; Suffolk 5m at 4.55. Weighted average = (21.75+22.75+27.50+21.75+21.50+43.00+22.75)/40 = 4.525%."},{"key":"central_beds_5_50","value":"\u00a35.0m to Central Bedfordshire at 5.50%, 17 March 2026 to 12 June 2026 (roughly three months)","as_at":"2026-03-17","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Appendix 1 (p.14). Bank Rate had been at 3.75% since 31 December 2025 (paragraph 4.1). 175 basis points over Bank Rate for three-month LA-to-LA money is a notable rate point for the sector-wide picture."},{"key":"lgr_merger","value":"MHCLG approved a merger of Basingstoke and Deane, Hart and Rushmoor on 25 March 2026, effective 1 April 2028","as_at":"2026-03-25","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Paragraph 9.1 (p.9), verbatim."},{"key":"section_9_amendment","value":"the paper asks Council on 16 July 2026 to remove the \u00a310m individual counterparty limit for lending to Hart or Rushmoor (\u00a350m aggregate LA-lending limit unchanged) and to raise the 'Prudential Limit for Long-Term Principal Investments to Mature' from \u00a320m to \u00a350m for 2027/28 and 2028/29","as_at":"2026-07-16","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Paragraphs 9.2 and 9.3 (p.9), verbatim. Loans to Hart or Rushmoor would not extend beyond April 2028 due to the merger. This is pre-merger treasury preparation \u2014 the debt-free investor using its ceilings to support the two merging partners before balance sheets combine."},{"key":"pooled_fund_carrying_position","value":"external pooled funds carried at \u00a322.64m vs cost \u00a325.00m \u2014 \u00a32.36m unrealised deficit \u2014 IFRS 9 statutory override to 2029/30","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"Table 3 (p.7): Royal London Short Dated Credit \u00a314.03m vs \u00a315m cost; CCLA Property \u00a38.61m vs \u00a310m cost. Overall funds rose \u00a30.03m during 2025/26 (paras 6.3, 6.5). Not a lead-with figure \u2014 small in absolute terms and inside statutory override to 1 April 2029 (para 6.2)."},{"key":"cfr_and_balance_sheet_identity","value":"CFR not published in this paper \u2014 balance-sheet identity cannot be tested from this document","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management 2025/26 Annual Report \u2014 Council, 16 July 2026","url":"https://democracy.basingstoke.gov.uk/documents/s500004333/treasury-management-annual-report-2025-26.pdf","doc_id":500004333,"drive_id":null,"text":"papers/E07000084/500004333.txt"},"note":"The paper is scoped to investments \u2014 Basingstoke and Deane treats treasury as an investment-only function because it carries no external debt. CFR would sit in the Statement of Accounts 2025/26, which we do not hold. Coherent with the accompanying Capital Programme Outturn (doc 500004331) recommendation 6 (p.2) that 'the capital programme remains fully funded from the council's own resources up to 2029/30.'"}],"threads":[],"meetings":{},"summary":{"written":"15 August 2026","text":"<p>Basingstoke and Deane carries no external debt. Paragraph 3.4 states 'no treasury management borrowing was undertaken during 2025/26' and paragraph 12.3 confirms the council has no interest costs on treasury borrowing; our PWLB price book returns no borrower on any variant of the name. This is a treasury management annual report scoped to investments only.</p><p>The book is \u00a3114.56m at cost on 31 March 2026 (average \u00a3130.79m over the year), earned 4.34% against a 4.04% average Bank Rate, and the internally managed portion earned 4.23% against a MUFG benchmark of 4.00% on a 228-council sample \u2014 with a 263-day weighted average maturity against a 52-day benchmark. A deliberately long book that has been paid off in a falling-rate year.</p><p>The distinctive feature is Section 9. On 25 March 2026 MHCLG approved a merger of Basingstoke and Deane, Hart and Rushmoor effective 1 April 2028. The paper asks Council on 16 July to remove the \u00a310m individual counterparty limit for lending to Hart or Rushmoor and to raise the Prudential Limit for Long-Term Principal Investments to Mature from \u00a320m to \u00a350m for 2027/28 and 2028/29. The debt-free investor is opening its ceilings to lend into the two councils it will merge with. Basingstoke and Deane is already lending \u00a340m to seven other authorities at 4.30%\u20135.50%, including a \u00a35m short-dated loan to Central Bedfordshire at 5.50% for 91 days from 17 March 2026 \u2014 a distinctive intra-LA rate point.</p>"},"courses":{"scored":false,"read":["treasury_annual"],"text_held":true},"contacts":[{"name":"Adam Swain","role":"Chief Finance Officer (Section 151 Officer)","email":"adam.swain@basingstoke.gov.uk","note":"Lead Officer on doc 500004333 (p.1); no address on this paper but address is stated on doc 500004331 (Capital Programme Outturn) at p.1, which we hold. Title verbatim from doc 500004333."},{"name":"Mark Jewers","role":"Technical Analyst","email":"mark.jewers@basingstoke.gov.uk","note":"Contact Officer on doc 500004333 (p.1) with address. Legitimate cc alongside the S151."},{"name":"Sarah Finch","role":"Accountancy Manager and Deputy S151 Officer","email":"sarah.finch@basingstoke.gov.uk","note":"Named on doc 500004331 (p.1). Title verbatim. Deputy S151 \u2014 reasonable cc but not required."}],"status":{"state":"ready-to-send","written_up":true,"contact":true,"as_at":"15 August 2026","note":"Read from local PDF. Paper text at papers/E07000084/500004333.txt. PWLB reconciliation TIES: no borrower on Basingstoke, Basingstoke and Deane, or Deane in our price book, and paragraphs 3.4 and 12.3 confirm the council carries no treasury borrowing. Balance-sheet identity cannot be run \u2014 paper publishes no CFR, no external borrowing, no other long-term liabilities. Investment book adds up: Chart 1 to \u00a3114.56m, Appendix 1 to \u00a3114,559,164, all sub-totals tie. DRAFT WRITTEN AND MACHINE-CHECKED. Officer address available (S151 Adam Swain on adam.swain@basingstoke.gov.uk from the paired capital paper we hold; Technical Analyst Mark Jewers on mark.jewers@basingstoke.gov.uk from this paper's front sheet)."},"emails":{"officer":{"subject":"\u00a340m already out to six councils, and Section 9 raises the ceiling for Hart and Rushmoor","to":"Adam Swain, Chief Finance Officer (Section 151 Officer) <adam.swain@basingstoke.gov.uk>","cc":"Mark Jewers, Technical Analyst <mark.jewers@basingstoke.gov.uk>","written":"15 August 2026","send_by":"2026-09-18","meeting":"no next treasury meeting stated in the paper","state":"ready-to-send","market":{"as_at":"2026-08-14","pwlb_notice":"314/26 (PM fixing)","basis":"MATURITY profile, CERTAINTY rates (published standard less 0.20)","pwlb_1y":4.95,"pwlb_2y":5.06,"pwlb_5y":5.34,"pwlb_10y":5.83,"pwlb_20y":6.38,"pwlb_25y":6.46,"pwlb_50y":6.27,"bank_rate":3.75,"ois_1y":4.11,"ois_2y":4.21,"july_activity":"\u00a3368m across 33 loans from 19 borrowers, average 5.02%","loan_file":"/api/data/bc-loans, sheet edited 2026-08-03"},"voice":"EMAIL_TEMPLATE.md","contacts_source":"S151 Adam Swain (title verbatim from doc 500004333 p.1; address from paired doc 500004331 p.1). CC Mark Jewers (contact officer with address on doc 500004333 p.1).","placeholders":["[BROCHURE URL]"],"links":["pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity","pwlbtoday.org/loan-analysis","pwlbtoday.org/academy"],"courses_named":["Devolution & reorganisation: the treasury view","Intra-LA borrowing & lending markets","Liability Benchmark: the essentials","PWLB break costs \u2014 mechanics & live calculator"],"reconciliation":"Our PWLB price book returns no borrower for Basingstoke and Deane on any variant of the name. That matches paragraph 3.4 ('No treasury management borrowing was undertaken during 2025/26') and paragraph 12.3 ('The council did not have any interest costs related to treasury management borrowing'). Nothing to reconcile because there is nothing there \u2014 on our data and on your paper alike, the council carries no PWLB.","sources":["papers/E07000084/500004333.txt","papers/E07000084/500004331.txt","/api/data/bc-loans"],"body":"Hi Adam,\n\nApologies for the cold email. PWLBtoday is fairly new so you may not have come across us yet.\n\nWe're built for local authority treasury teams and nothing else: live PWLB rates at both daily fixings, a monthly breakdown of every loan the sector draws, every council's PWLB book priced daily against the DMO's repayment curve, and a training academy for officers and members. There's more \u2014 the predictor, the SONIA path, peer metrics, a daily brief \u2014 in the brochure at [BROCHURE URL]. We don't sell treasury products, and the data side is free and stays free.\n\nTo keep that data and our training relevant we built tools that watch committee papers across every council in the country, so we can see how a council's own figures compare with where the market has gone since. So below are a few thoughts on the Treasury Management Annual Report that went to Council on 16 July. You're not being singled out \u2014 every authority gets one of these \u2014 and there's no agenda in it.\n\nA cross-check first, which came out unusually clean. Our PWLB price book returns no borrower on any variant of Basingstoke, Basingstoke and Deane, or Deane. That ties with your paragraph 3.4 (\"no treasury management borrowing was undertaken during 2025/26\") and paragraph 12.3 (\"the council did not have any interest costs related to treasury management borrowing\"). There is nothing to reconcile because there is nothing there \u2014 one of a small handful of councils in our library that carry no PWLB and no other external debt either. Worth naming in case the fact is ever useful.\n\nThe part of the paper that struck us is Section 9. The MHCLG merger approval of 25 March, effective 1 April 2028, is a piece of context every treasury reader knows about, but the treasury shape you're asking Council to authorise on the back of it is distinctive. Removing the \u00a310m individual counterparty cap for lending to Hart or Rushmoor while holding the \u00a350m aggregate LA-lending limit, and raising the Long-Term Principal Investments to Mature limit from \u00a320m to \u00a350m for 2027/28 and 2028/29 \u2014 this is a debt-free investor deliberately widening its ceilings to be able to lend into the two councils it's about to combine with. We don't see that shape often; most pre-merger treasury preparation we've read is about aligning debt strategies, not about the investor authority stepping up as a lender inside the group.\n\nWhich connects to what we might genuinely be useful for. Basingstoke and Deane is already lending \u00a340m across seven loans to six councils at rates between 4.30% and 5.50%, so you're on the lender side of the intra-LA market rather than the borrower side. That market is the one nobody publishes \u2014 every council sees its own trades and none sees the distribution. We track it across the sector, so what other authorities are actually paying and offering for one-month, three-month, six-month and one-year money is visible rather than inferred. For context on the borrowing side, July's PWLB lending was \u00a3368m across 33 loans from just 19 borrowers at an average 5.02% \u2014 a thin month, which changes what deposit-side rates you're likely to be offered. That breakdown is at pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity.\n\nOne data point from your own file that we noticed. Your \u00a35m to Central Bedfordshire at 5.50% from 17 March to 12 June is a striking short-dated rate to have caught \u2014 Bank Rate had been at 3.75% since 31 December on your own paragraph 4.1. 175 basis points over Bank Rate for three-month LA-to-LA money sits at the top end of what we're seeing for that tenor across the councils we've read, and it's a useful reference point for how the ceiling of that market prices when a council needs cash. We publish daily rate ranges rather than a single mid, precisely so a trade like that has somewhere to sit next to.\n\nOn training, four fit. Devolution & reorganisation: the treasury view is the most obvious given April 2028, and lands well with authorities carrying pre-merger positions. Intra-LA borrowing & lending markets covers the lender side you're already on. Liability Benchmark: the essentials, which is the indicator that answers \"how long can we keep not borrowing\" \u2014 trivially satisfied today but a useful frame for the merged authority's day one. And PWLB break costs \u2014 mechanics & live calculator, if you're going to inherit debt from Hart or Rushmoor on merger day. All 115 courses are free to local authorities until the end of September at pwlbtoday.org/academy, and free accounts for the whole team.\n\nAnd if you do find it useful, mentioning it to others would help us more than anything. We're small, word of mouth is most of our marketing, and every sign-up helps us prove the need.\n\nSign up free \u2192 https://pwlbtoday.org/\nFollow on LinkedIn \u2192 https://www.linkedin.com/company/pwlbtoday/\n\nRussell\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/in/russelltew\n\nSource: Treasury Management Annual Report 2025/26 and Capital Programme Outturn Report 2025/26, Council, 16 July 2026."}}}}